WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.600
SpaceX flew three rockets in a single day, placing astronauts on the ISS, a Google AI test satellite

00:00:06.600 --> 00:00:14.780
in orbit, and a classified NRO payload. This is SpaceX Daily, episode 118. On Thursday,

00:00:14.880 --> 00:00:20.440
the company sent four astronauts to the International Space Station aboard Crew 13 from Cape

00:00:20.440 --> 00:00:26.040
Canaveral Space Force Station. A separate falcon 9 lifted off from vandenberg and placed

00:00:26.040 --> 00:00:31.260
Google's project's Suncatcher prototype into orbit, carrying tensor processing units.

00:00:31.900 --> 00:00:37.180
The same day, a Falcon Heavy departed Florida with the first NRO spy satellite mission.

00:00:37.940 --> 00:00:41.580
Every payload reached its planned orbit, despite the compressed schedule.

00:00:42.260 --> 00:00:48.480
Earlier in the week, Starship completed its first orbital flight and released 26 Starlink V3 satellites,

00:00:49.100 --> 00:00:51.240
even after losing one engine on a scent.

00:00:51.240 --> 00:00:56.800
According to ours, Technica, those three flights in roughly 13 hours marked one of the

00:00:56.800 --> 00:01:03.220
busiest operational days in company history. SpaceX has filed plans with the FCC for as many

00:01:03.220 --> 00:01:08.800
as 1 million satellites under the Star-Mind Orbital Data Center project. The first satellites

00:01:08.800 --> 00:01:16.080
could fly as soon as 2027 from the new Gigasat factory in Bastrop, Texas. Each AI-1 satellite

00:01:16.080 --> 00:01:22.740
will carry two large solar panels and radiators sized for an average 120 kilowatts of compute power.

00:01:23.160 --> 00:01:28.820
The application lists the full constellation at up to 1 million spacecraft. Heat rejection

00:01:28.820 --> 00:01:35.000
in vacuum is listed as the main remaining engineering limit. The company has already signed multi-billion

00:01:35.000 --> 00:01:40.300
dollar contracts with Google, Anthropic, and other customers for future orbital capacity.

00:01:40.300 --> 00:01:45.520
Per the filing details, the design draws on thermal management experience from more

00:01:45.520 --> 00:01:48.160
than 10,000 operational Starlink satellites.

00:01:48.920 --> 00:01:56.420
XAI plans to bring roughly 420,000 additional NVIDIA GPUs online at the Memphis Colossus

00:01:56.420 --> 00:01:57.440
cluster in November.

00:01:58.060 --> 00:02:06.100
The two current sites together hold about 780,000 chips, with Colossus 1 running 150,000 H-100s,

00:02:06.100 --> 00:02:10.660
50,000 H-200s and 30,000 GB-200s.

00:02:11.140 --> 00:02:16.840
The November edition is part of the larger push toward 1.44 million GPUs by the end of

00:02:16.840 --> 00:02:17.200
the year.

00:02:17.900 --> 00:02:22.820
Most of Colossus 1 capacity is already leased to Anthropic under agreements that could

00:02:22.820 --> 00:02:26.380
total $84.5 billion by 2029.

00:02:27.180 --> 00:02:31.620
A new 1.2 gigawatt power plant is under construction to support the growth.

00:02:31.620 --> 00:02:36.460
Both. According to crypto briefing, the Ramp will serve both internal grok training

00:02:36.790 --> 00:02:42.660
and external customer workloads. Ryan Mattal and Argo Tech placed their first joint satellite

00:02:42.660 --> 00:02:49.100
into orbit on a Falcon 9. The defense-focused payload flew on the Transporter-18 Rideshare

00:02:49.100 --> 00:02:54.860
mission and reached its target orbit without issue. The launch marks the two companies' first

00:02:54.860 --> 00:03:00.800
operational spacecraft together. Abu Dhabi's first commercial AI satellite also launched

00:03:00.800 --> 00:03:06.860
on the same Falcon 9. The spacecraft is testing orbital data processing and wireless energy

00:03:06.860 --> 00:03:12.840
beaming concepts. It shared the ride with Google's TPU prototype. Per circuit news,

00:03:13.180 --> 00:03:17.660
the mission gives the United Arab Emirates its initial on-orbit compute demonstration.

00:03:18.380 --> 00:03:23.720
Several new satellites on Transporter 18 are evaluating orbital data centers and energy

00:03:23.720 --> 00:03:30.380
beaming hardware. The mission carried 130 payloads in total to Sun Synchronous Orbit. Google

00:03:30.380 --> 00:03:35.600
has already confirmed contact with its prototype and reports that initial systems are performing

00:03:35.600 --> 00:03:42.000
as expected. Project Senior Director Travis Beals noted the satellite is gathering the first orbital

00:03:42.000 --> 00:03:48.820
data on radiation, thermal extremes, and heat rejection. According to Axios, the test will run for

00:03:48.820 --> 00:03:54.340
several weeks before returning performance metrics. Starlink mini-dish prices have reached their

00:03:54.340 --> 00:04:00.280
lowest level yet ahead of the Mini-2 launch. The reduction is intended to broaden consumer access

00:04:00.280 --> 00:04:05.800
before the next hardware revision arrives later this year. Notebook Check reports the timing

00:04:05.800 --> 00:04:12.040
aligns with continued network capacity growth from recent Starship and Falcon flights. Elon Musk

00:04:12.040 --> 00:04:17.500
clarified that recent remarks on Starship flight rates do not signal any reduction in the company's

00:04:17.500 --> 00:04:19.080
long-term cadence targets.

00:04:19.680 --> 00:04:22.960
The statement addresses market readings of earlier comments.

00:04:23.500 --> 00:04:28.840
SpaceX continues to work toward rapid flight rates once remaining regulatory and technical

00:04:28.840 --> 00:04:30.020
milestones are cleared.

00:04:30.740 --> 00:04:35.380
Barents carried the clarification on the same day the stock traded higher after the triple

00:04:35.380 --> 00:04:35.820
launch.

00:04:36.720 --> 00:04:41.720
Observers on the Space Coast reported multiple sonic booms during the Falcon Heavy launch window

00:04:41.720 --> 00:04:42.480
near midnight.

00:04:42.480 --> 00:04:47.360
videos posted online captured the distinctive triple boom sequence from the

00:04:47.360 --> 00:04:53.320
three-core vehicle. W-E-S-H noted the booms were heard across Brevard County as

00:04:53.320 --> 00:04:59.000
the rocket climbed on the NRO mission. Kyrgyz Republic President Sadir Japarov

00:04:59.000 --> 00:05:04.260
watched a Falcon 9 launch from Vandenberg Space Force Base. The visit underscores

00:05:04.260 --> 00:05:09.480
rising international interest in US commercial launch infrastructure. Vandenberg

00:05:09.480 --> 00:05:13.980
space for his base posted details of the president's attendance at the ride share mission.

00:05:14.820 --> 00:05:20.220
Some viewers of the Starship live stream speculated about UFOs after unusual light patterns

00:05:20.220 --> 00:05:25.860
appeared during a cent. SpaceX confirmed the patterns were caused by engine plume interactions

00:05:25.860 --> 00:05:31.240
with the upper atmosphere. The clarification came after the orbital test flight that deployed

00:05:31.240 --> 00:05:38.420
the first Starling V3 satellites. A golden dragon plush served as crew 13's 0G indicator inside

00:05:38.420 --> 00:05:44.860
the dragon capsule. The toy nicknamed Lucky became visible to the crew shortly after orbital insertion.

00:05:45.600 --> 00:05:50.720
Collect Space reports the choice carried a nod to Canadian astronaut Joshua Kutrick on the mission.

00:05:51.680 --> 00:05:57.120
Florida today published photos of the Falcon Heavy climbing against the night sky from Kennedy Space Center.

00:05:57.800 --> 00:06:02.940
The images highlight the distinctive triple core configuration during the classified NRO launch.

00:06:02.940 --> 00:06:09.280
One thing worth watching is the environmental monitoring push around XAI data centers in Memphis.

00:06:10.020 --> 00:06:18.660
University of Memphis professor Chun-Rong-Gia is calling for expanded air and water quality tracking as the Colossus cluster scales.

00:06:19.280 --> 00:06:27.100
The concerned centers on power plant emissions and cooling water use tied to the 1.2 gigawatt facility now under construction.

00:06:27.100 --> 00:06:31.540
addition. Additional data collection would clarify whether current permits adequately

00:06:31.540 --> 00:06:35.660
address cumulative impacts from the Rapid GPU expansion.

00:06:36.520 --> 00:06:42.500
On the AI front, Google confirmed contact with its Project Suncatcher TPU satellite and

00:06:42.500 --> 00:06:49.520
reported nominal initial operations after the Transporter 18 deployment. SpaceX AI intends

00:06:49.520 --> 00:06:57.040
to bring 420,000 more NVIDIA GPUs online in November as part of the Memphis Colossus expansion

00:06:57.040 --> 00:07:03.520
The ramp supports both internal groc training and external leases, including the anthropic agreement.

00:07:04.380 --> 00:07:09.100
Abu Dhabi's first commercial AI satellite also reached orbit on the same mission,

00:07:09.620 --> 00:07:12.660
testing in-space compute and energy beaming concepts.

00:07:13.480 --> 00:07:19.420
Engineering deep dive on why placing tensor processing units on small dedicated satellites changes

00:07:19.420 --> 00:07:25.320
the cost and performance math for orbital compute. In low-earth orbit, a satellite receives

00:07:25.320 --> 00:07:30.080
near continuous sunlight, delivering up to 8 times the solar energy per square meter,

00:07:30.580 --> 00:07:32.420
compared with most terrestrial sites.

00:07:33.100 --> 00:07:38.240
The Google prototype therefore carries oversized radiators, sized for vacuum heat rejection.

00:07:38.820 --> 00:07:43.140
A constraint that does not exist when air or liquid cooling is available on the ground.

00:07:43.800 --> 00:07:49.340
If the raw material cost of the solar arrays and radiators is treated as the magic wand baseline,

00:07:49.340 --> 00:07:54.680
line, the finished satellite still carries an idiot index several times higher because

00:07:54.680 --> 00:07:59.680
of radiation-hardening, attitude control, and laser cross-links required for data return.

00:08:00.420 --> 00:08:05.180
That multiplier shrinks only when launch costs fall enough that an entire constellation

00:08:05.180 --> 00:08:09.340
can be refreshed every few years instead of designed for decade-long life.

00:08:09.920 --> 00:08:15.520
The same physics explains why SpaceX's own Star-Mind concept pairs large solar arrays

00:08:15.520 --> 00:08:22.460
with radiators on each AI-1 satellite, targeting 120 kilowatts average compute power.

00:08:22.980 --> 00:08:26.680
Continuous solar input removes the terrestrial grid bottleneck,

00:08:26.980 --> 00:08:31.500
but vacuum heat rejection remains the binding limit on compute density per kilogram.

00:08:32.340 --> 00:08:35.580
Early data from the current test will therefore directly inform

00:08:35.580 --> 00:08:40.120
whether 120 kilowatt-class nodes can be scaled to thousands of units,

00:08:40.600 --> 00:08:43.300
without exceeding acceptable mass or power budgets

00:08:43.300 --> 00:08:49.820
for the planned 1 million satellite constellation. The 1.2 gigawatt ground plant under construction

00:08:49.820 --> 00:08:55.640
for Colossus in Memphis follows the identical constraint on Earth where power delivery and cooling

00:08:55.640 --> 00:09:03.500
already limit the Novenger ramp of 420,000 additional GPUs toward the 1.44 million total target.

00:09:04.220 --> 00:09:12.340
A quick note on the markets. SPCX closed at $157.27 up 5.6%.

00:09:12.340 --> 00:09:18.200
Before we go, keep an eye on the first performance numbers expected from Google's TPU satellite

00:09:18.200 --> 00:09:19.260
in the coming weeks.

